---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../../styles/service.css";

const SITE = "https://www.tmdesignengineering.com";
const url = `${SITE}/work/ooni-karu-2`;
const jsonld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "@id": `${url}#article`,
    headline: "Ooni Karu 2, combustion, the ClearView™ anti-sooting door, and door DFMA",
    description: "Case study: performance lead and design engineer for the Ooni Karu 2 door and multi-fuel combustion, the patented ClearView™ passive anti-sooting geometry, a high-temperature magnetic latch, and a door DFMA that cut BOM by around 70% and assembly from a day to under two minutes. iF and German Design Award 2024.",
    url,
    author: { "@id": `${SITE}/#thomas-mckee` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${SITE}/#business` },
    about: "Ooni Karu 2 multi-fuel pizza oven",
    isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE}/#website` },
    sameAs: [
      "https://ifdesign.com/en/winner-ranking/project/ooni-karu-12g-multi-fuel-pizza-oven/643103",
      "https://ifdesign.com/en/winner-ranking/project/ooni-karu-2-pro/709204",
    ],
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Work", item: `${SITE}/#work` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Ooni Karu 2", item: url },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Ooni Karu 2 Case Study, ClearView™ Door, Combustion & DFMA | Thomas McKee"
  description="Case study: performance lead and design engineer for the Ooni Karu 2 door, the patented ClearView™ passive anti-sooting geometry, a 500 °C magnetic latch, and a door DFMA that cut BOM around 70% and assembly from over a day to under two minutes. iF & German Design Award 2024."
  type="website"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
<main>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="svc-hero">
    <div class="wrap svc-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Case Study · Ooni Karu 2</span>
        <h1>The Karu 2 door, <em>a clear view of the fire, for a fraction of the cost.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">I was performance lead and design engineer for the door and combustion of Ooni's multi-fuel oven. The headline is a patented door that stays clear through a full wood cook using nothing but geometry, no added parts, a larger window, and a bill of materials cut by around 70%. It won <a href="https://ifdesign.com/en/winner-ranking/project/ooni-karu-12g-multi-fuel-pizza-oven/643103" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color:var(--ember)">iF</a> and German Design Awards in 2024, and its architecture was carried onto the <a href="https://ifdesign.com/en/winner-ranking/project/ooni-karu-2-pro/709204" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color:var(--ember)">Karu 2 Pro (iF 2025)</a>.</p>
        <div class="svc-hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#contact">Start your project <span class="arw">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/#work">More work</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="spec" aria-label="Project summary">
        <div class="spec-head"><span>Project</span><span>CASE 02</span></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Client</div><div class="spec-v">Ooni</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Role</div><div class="spec-v">Performance lead · design engineer</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Owned</div><div class="spec-v">Door · latch · fuel &amp; airflow</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Awards</div><div class="spec-v">iF · German Design 2024</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Patents</div><div class="spec-v">ClearView™ · removable door</div></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HEADLINE -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="headline">
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">~70%</div><div class="hl">door BOM cost, with a larger window</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">&lt;2 min</div><div class="hl">door assembly time, from over 24 hours</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">−27%</div><div class="hl">solid fuel consumption vs the previous generation</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">4.7★</div><div class="hl">across 1,700+ customer reviews</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 2.1 COMBUSTION - PROBLEM -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Problem</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.1 · Combustion &amp; airflow</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Optimising combustion and cooking performance across multiple fuels</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch">
        <p>A multi-fuel oven has to burn wood <em>and</em> gas well on the same <strong>combustion and airflow</strong> setup, and the two have almost opposite requirements: solid fuel wants a deep bed, primary air underneath and a long residence time, whereas gas needs controlled entrainment for a stable flame front.</p>
        <p>On top of that, the Karu 2 was gaining a large glass viewing window where the previous generation had insulated steel. Glass radiates and conducts heat straight out of the cavity, so the combustion system had to recoup that loss before it could make any improvement at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout"><b>Targets</b>: 500&nbsp;°C stone with a 60-second cook · even flame across the chamber · lower solid-fuel consumption than the previous generation · stable performance in wind and rain · touch-temperature and emissions compliance.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 2.1 COMBUSTION - SOLUTION -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase phase-solution">Solution</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.1 · Combustion &amp; airflow</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">A thermodynamic model and CFD for the air path, and a handle sized by hand before FEA</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch">
        <p><strong>CFD</strong> was used to study the area and position of the primary inlet. I tested with blanking plates over the inlet and chimney to optimise the airflow for both fast heat-up and low-temperature holding, while accounting for conditions such as wind and rain ingress.</p>
        <p>The baffle handle is a rod running from a 500&nbsp;°C chamber to a surface the user must grip. Before simulating anything I sized it as a fin by <strong>hand calculation</strong>: steady one-dimensional conduction with convective loss along the length and at the tip sets how fast the tip temperature falls, and that depends only on the handle's diameter, section and material. This informed the length, thickness and standoff of the shaft, so a thin stainless shaft with a deliberate thermal break puts the grip inside the standard's <strong>touch-temperature compliance</strong> limits. <strong>FEA</strong> and instrumented testing confirmed it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/karu-thermal.png" alt="Instrumented touch-temperature testing of the baffle handle with thermocouples" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 43, Instrumented touch-temperature testing of the baffle handle. [image to swap]</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/karu-clearview-fire.png" alt="Combustion simulation of the flame and airflow across the chamber" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 44, Combustion simulation of the flame and air path across the chamber. [image to swap]</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 2.1 COMBUSTION - RESULTS -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Results</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.1 · Combustion &amp; airflow</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">A more efficient oven even with a glass door</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch">
        <p>Combustion efficiency improvements cut solid fuel consumption to 27% less than the previous generation, while a 45% larger fuel tray gave a broader, more even flame and better coverage of the stone. The oven still reaches 500&nbsp;°C with a 60-second cook despite the radiant and conductive loss through a large window.</p>
        <p>The new baffle handle passes touch temperature with margin, unlike the hot handle of the previous model. I owned the pre-compliance and DVP testing end to end, documentation included, and the final baffle design was re-used on the larger Karu 2 Pro.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="headline" style="margin-top:26px;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)">
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">−27%</div><div class="hl">solid fuel consumption against the previous generation</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">+45%</div><div class="hl">solid-fuel tray area, for a broader, more even flame</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">500 °C</div><div class="hl">60-second cook, achieved even with the large glass window</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 2.2 CLEARVIEW -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Problem</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.2 · ClearView™ door</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Designing and integrating a glass door to view cooking food</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch">
        <p>The original plan was a small peephole, so a user could glimpse the pizza without a large window sooting up. There wasn't budget for the airwash technology used on the flagship Karu 16, and that technology was unreliable and would have covered most of the viewing space on this model anyway. Soot deposits on glass because incompletely burned carbon particles reach a surface cool enough for them to stick, so the design had to keep particle-laden gas off the glass without a complex structure in front of it.</p>
        <p>The previous generation's anti-soot architecture was expensive in every sense: it blocked 30% of the glass, ran to 30+ parts and 40+ fixings in the door, took over 24&nbsp;hours of assembly and cure time, and soot was <em>still</em> an issue.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="callout"><b>Constraints on the door</b>: no added components · no added assembly time · no reduction in window area · must work on wood, charcoal and gas · must survive 500&nbsp;°C and thermal cycling · strict industrial-design intent on the visible surfaces, and a lower budget.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase phase-solution">Solution</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.2 · ClearView™ door</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Recessing the glass and allowing passive cool air to create a turbulent boundary layer</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch">
        <p>Soot arrives at the glass by thermophoresis and diffusion across the boundary layer: a thick, quiescent layer lets particles drift in and settle. Deliberately tripping the flow along the inside face of the glass thins that layer and sweeps particles back into the bulk gas, where they burn. The mechanism is pure <strong>geometric design</strong>, recess the glass and route passive cool air across it, so it adds nothing to cost or assembly.</p>
        <p>I hand-prototyped it: <strong>sheet-metal</strong> and borosilicate doors tested on old ovens, each iteration run to a full wood cook and judged on the glass. Early prototypes were still sooty, the air path was in the right place, but the aim and the gap were wrong. Successive iterations moved the entry, changed the angle of attack along the glass and closed the gap until the flow stayed attached across the whole window rather than just its lower half.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <figure class="fig wide"><img src="/work/karu-clearview-fire.png" alt="Flames clearly visible through the clear glass door of the Karu 2 during a wood cook" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 52, The final anti-sooting geometry: flames stay clearly visible through the glass across a full wood cook.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Results</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.2 · ClearView™ door</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Clear glass, a larger window, cheaper BOM and running costs</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch">
        <p>The geometry solved the problem outright: soot deposits visibly on the surrounding metal while the glass stays clear through a full wood cook. Because both the anti-sooting and the airflow are geometry, the invention is effectively free, nothing added to the bill of materials, nothing added to the line, and the window ended up <em>larger</em> than the original brief allowed. The peephole was never needed. There's a second-order win too: on gas the flame stays visible through the glass, so the door stays shut, where the previous door had to be removed to see the flame and lost a chunk of the oven's efficiency every time.</p>
        <p>ClearView™ is patented, and the architecture scaled: it was carried onto the larger Karu 2 Pro (iF Design Award 2025), where the bigger window and better performance came with the BOM and assembly savings intact.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/karu-clearview-life.png" alt="Two people looking through the clear glass door of a Karu 2 oven outdoors" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 54, Tooled prototype: soot deposits on the surrounding metal while the glass stays clear.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/karu-thermal.png" alt="Thermal camera image of the door surface reading around 57 degrees Celsius" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 55, Karu 2 Pro, inheriting the architecture: only seven parts and two fixings.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 2.3 DFMA -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Problem</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.3 · Door DFMA &amp; magnetic latch</span></div>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The door performing in high-performance conditions, including magnets at 500 °C</h2>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch">
        <p>The previous door took over 24&nbsp;hours of assembly time per unit, largely curing a gasket, and ran to more than 70 parts and fasteners. The replacement had to be cheaper, faster, and hold the <strong>industrial-design intent</strong> on every visible surface.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-row" style="margin-top:30px"><span class="phase phase-solution">Solution</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.3 · Door DFMA &amp; magnetic latch</span></div>
      <div class="about-body" style="max-width:70ch;margin-top:14px">
        <p>Through <strong>DFMA</strong> I resolved the <strong>cast parts</strong> to a uniform wall with draft and release on a single line of draw; pressed the <strong>sheet-metal</strong> parts with generous flow radii and pressed-in features rather than welds; got the whole door down to two fixings, both hidden; and located the glass on tabbed, deliberately compliant brackets so assembly can't over-constrain and crack the glass. Deleting the gasket in favour of compressive high-temperature parts removed the cure time entirely.</p>
        <p>The door is held shut by magnets that must hold at temperature and release by hand. Magnet grade falls as it heats, so I placed the magnets where the door runs coolest and reduced the conduction path into them, then sized the hold from a moment balance about the hinge line by <strong>hand calculation</strong>, including a wind-gust term and the <em>derated</em> pull at the magnet's working temperature, not its room-temperature rating. Pull-off was then measured empirically, hot. The latch is also serviceable: magnets can be replaced without destroying the door, which matters on a product with a five-year field life outdoors. <strong>Materials selection</strong> for extreme heat, UV and rain was verified in simulation with heat shields, and the door was drop-tested; a fully removable door was designed, <strong>user-tested</strong> and patented.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="figs">
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/karu-door-sketch.png" alt="Hand sketch of the Karu 2 door design with annotations on draft, fixings and manufacturability" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 56, Magnet placement and the moment-balance calculation about the hinge line.</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/karu-mould.png" alt="Injection mould tooling for the door parts on a factory floor" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 57, Shipped door detail. [image to swap]</figcaption></figure>
        <figure class="fig"><img src="/work/karu-caliper.png" alt="Digital calipers measuring an incoming door part during inspection" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fig. 58, Incoming-part inspection during supplier and tooling support.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="phase-row" style="margin-top:30px"><span class="phase">Results</span><span class="phase-cs">Case study 2.3 · Door DFMA &amp; magnetic latch</span></div>
      <div class="headline" style="margin-top:14px;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)">
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">~70%</div><div class="hl">door BOM reduction, with a larger window</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">&lt;2 min</div><div class="hl">assembly, from over 24 hours</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">2 products</div><div class="hl">shipped on the architecture, Karu 2 and Karu 2 Pro</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW I HELP -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">How I could help you</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Better and cheaper, deliberately</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Finding the change that improves the product and takes out cost is the heart of <a href="/services/manufacturing-preparation" style="color:var(--ember)">Manufacturing Preparation</a> and <a href="/services/design-verification" style="color:var(--ember)">Design Verification</a>. If you've a design that works but costs too much to build, or a hard thermal, combustion or mechanical detail to crack, that's where I'm most useful.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="start">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta">
        <div class="inner">
          <span class="eyebrow">Let's talk</span>
          <h2 style="margin-top:14px">Design that's good and buildable</h2>
          <p>Book a free intro call and tell me where your product is. If it needs to be better, cheaper or more manufacturable, ideally all three, let's talk.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/#contact">Book a free intro call <span class="arw">→</span></a>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href="/#work">See more work</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

<style>
  .headline{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:1px;background:var(--line);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden}
  @media(max-width:760px){.headline{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}}
  .hstat{background:var(--surface);padding:22px 20px}
  .hn{font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:2rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-0.03em;color:var(--ink)}
  .hl{color:var(--ink-2);font-size:.9rem;margin-top:6px;line-height:1.4}
  .callout{border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-left:3px solid var(--ember);background:var(--surface);border-radius:4px;padding:14px 18px;margin-top:24px;font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:13px;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.6;max-width:72ch}
  .callout b{color:var(--ink)}
  .figs{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:16px;margin-top:28px}
  @media(max-width:640px){.figs{grid-template-columns:1fr}}
  .fig{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--surface);margin:0}
  .fig.wide{grid-column:1/-1}
  .fig img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}
  .fig figcaption{font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-3);padding:9px 12px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);line-height:1.5}
</style>
</Base>
